<article class="env-remark" id="02DF31">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02DF31">Remark <span data-tag="02DF31">5.5.4.10</span>.</a> Let $F: \calC ' \rightarrow \calC $ be a functor between $\infty $-categories. Suppose that $\calC $ and $\calC '$ admit small colimits and that $F$ preserves small colimits. Let $S$ be a strongly saturated class of morphisms in $\calC '$. Then $F^{-1} S$ is a strongly saturated class of morphisms of $\calC $. In particular, if we let $S$ denote the collection of all morphisms $f$ of $\calC '$ such that $F(f)$ is an equivalence, then $S$ is strongly saturated. </p>
